WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.420
This is the iconic opening
scene in "The Lion King."

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.900
Compared to the original,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.260
it's an almost shot-for-shot remake,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.530
but take a closer look at Rafiki.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.350
In the 1994 film he stands
while lifting the cub,

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.840
and in the new version he sits.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.040
That's because Rafiki is a mandrill,

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.740
an animal that couldn't actually stand

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.720
while lifting a lion cub.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.093
Striking that balance between

00:00:23.093 --> 00:00:24.940
staying true to the original film

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.257
and creating a
hyper-realistically animated

00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:29.165
"Lion King" for a new audience

00:00:29.165 --> 00:00:31.440
was a particularly tough challenge,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.763
and it required literally
millions of hours of animating.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.370
The visual-effects masters at MPC Film

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.590
helped animate this
ambitious live-action remake.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.620
Their fingerprints are all over films

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.010
that combine live action
and CGI animation,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.690
from "Dumbo" to "Detective Pikachu."

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.167
But it was their work on
another Disney remake,

00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:57.710
"The Jungle Book," that
really paved the way

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.840
for what they were able to
achieve with "The Lion King."

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.420
This is Elliot Newman,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:05.470
one of the VFX Supervisors
on "The Lion King."

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.780
If you want a sense of
just how much work Newman

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.640
and the MPC Film team
put into this project,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.482
take a look at a few stats.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.090
MPC Film estimates that there
were about 77 million hours

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.400
of rendering animation throughout
all of their departments.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.310
If they used a single computer,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.310
it would've taken 8,790
years to finish the movie.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.610
Before they could begin fully animating,

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.570
they had to actually shoot the movie,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.380
and the actors needed
to record their voices.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.350
Instead of just recording
their voices in a sound booth,

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.050
the actors were actually
able to move around,

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.280
as some scenes were recorded

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.620
in what is called a black box theater.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.550
This bare-bones setup gave the actors room

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.870
to interact with the
cast and even improvise.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:00.100
Some parts though were
recorded in a recording booth.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.890
Donald Glover: Mercy? After what you did?

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:04.560
Narrator: While not motion captured,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.370
the actors' performances were recorded

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.360
and used by animators
as a point of reference.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.050
The first steps in animation happen

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.630
in what's called pre-visualization.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.873
The animation team worked

00:02:14.873 --> 00:02:17.530
with animation supervisor Andy Jones

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.790
to create simplified animated sequences

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.300
that could be used for virtual reality.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.000
This VR approach on set,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.567
which they called virtual
production, was unique.

00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:29.353
Director Jon Favreau and
several crew members would put

00:02:29.353 --> 00:02:32.350
on VR headsets which allowed
them to actually step foot

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.295
in the virtual set they created.

00:02:34.295 --> 00:02:36.400
This way, they could set up shots,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.397
adjust lighting, and
choreograph movements.

00:02:39.397 --> 00:02:41.090
They treated this digital environment

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.490
like a place that really existed.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:46.350
The animals and environments
were created at the same time,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:48.560
and rough versions of the
animations could be viewed

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.040
on monitors while shooting took place.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.690
They would eventually add
more fully realized details

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.140
and other crucial aspects, like
lighting, after production.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.140
We'll get more into that later.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:04.920
And, yes, there were real cameras
tracking Timon And Pumbaa.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.330
Creating the camera
movements in real life,

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.340
rather than just in the
computer, allowed the filmmakers

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.460
to create a nature-documentary-like feel.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.210
When it came to animals,
they had to toe the fine line

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.362
between creating realistic animals

00:03:18.362 --> 00:03:20.730
and staying true to the original movie.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:22.113
Newman: Even though we were making

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:25.305
very realistic-looking lions,

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.960
we still needed to make sure
that when you look at Simba

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.447
you kind of resonate that that's Simba,

00:03:30.447 --> 00:03:31.720
and you understand that

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:32.730
that's the character you're looking at,

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.750
or, you know, if you're looking at Mufasa,

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.402
then he's got the right
visual style for you

00:03:37.402 --> 00:03:39.276
to kind of remember that that's, you know,

00:03:39.276 --> 00:03:43.325
he is the alpha lion and, you
know, he's larger than life

00:03:43.325 --> 00:03:45.218
and he has this presence
about him, you know.

00:03:45.218 --> 00:03:46.930
Narrator: The animals were a combination

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:51.930
of storyboards, sketches,
and exhaustive research.

00:03:52.078 --> 00:03:53.815
Favreau and the team

00:03:53.815 --> 00:03:55.488
took a trip to Africa,

00:03:55.488 --> 00:03:59.010
where they observed real animals
in their natural habitats,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.810
which helped them build the world,

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.260
plus they could get up close and personal

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:04.430
with a lot of the animals

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.600
at Disney's Animal Kingdom
in Orlando, Florida.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.530
They also read anatomy
books and science papers

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.350
and watched hours of documentaries

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.070
and other reference footage.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.480
Many of the animals the crew saw

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.830
in Kenya inspired the characters.

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.395
For example, an excited lion cub they saw

00:04:20.395 --> 00:04:23.073
running around as the
rest of the pride slept

00:04:23.073 --> 00:04:26.170
was a big influence on
how they animated Simba.

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.850
For some animals, making
them both realistic

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:32.200
as well as faithful to
the original wasn't easy.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.220
Perhaps the best example
of that is Rafiki.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.210
Newman: If you put him
against a real mandrill,

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.710
you'd see some differences.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.540
Narrator: Rafiki's most
famous moment comes

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.730
when he stands and lifts
Simba over his head

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.050
for all the Pride Lands to see.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:48.710
As we mentioned, mandrills
can't really stand up like that.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.550
However, the movie is shot
and edited in such a way

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.670
that you'll barely notice a difference.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.120
Because Rafiki is in the primate family,

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.580
he's the character most
similar to a human.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.345
Therefore, they were able to add

00:05:00.345 --> 00:05:03.170
some more humanlike qualities
and emotions to him.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.600
The animators, however,
took some liberties

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.570
and occasionally strayed
from realism in a few places.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:10.400
Take Scar, for example.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.341
His design was the furthest from reality

00:05:12.341 --> 00:05:14.233
compared to the other lions,

00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:15.600
but they made it work.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.090
He has a lot of physical qualities

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:19.090
that are different from an actual lion,

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.640
like his size and the shape of his skull.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:22.853
Newman: He feels more like a character

00:05:22.853 --> 00:05:23.949
than the rest of them,

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:25.335
but actually once he was in the shots

00:05:25.335 --> 00:05:27.727
and once we'd lit things and
we'd put the environment in

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.785
and his performance was
there, it was all animated,

00:05:29.785 --> 00:05:31.420
he worked in every shot.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.600
It was, you know, one of
the best characters we had.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.750
Narrator: The animators
also had the challenge

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.430
of making the animals not only realistic

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:40.310
but able to sing and talk.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.890
One change: They repositioned
some animals' heads

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.740
so the audience wouldn't
always have to stare

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.220
right into their mouths.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.140
They still had to make
sure their mouths moved

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.060
how they did in real life,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.690
so they timed the character's
breathing to their dialogue.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.513
Jones said they would
let the belly muscles

00:05:56.513 --> 00:05:58.398
and diaphragm tighten.

00:05:58.398 --> 00:06:00.548
That way, it felt like a given animal

00:06:00.548 --> 00:06:03.560
was forcing air out of
its mouth as they spoke.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.660
Each layer of these animals
went through simulations,

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.310
an additional phase of animation

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.910
where skin details,
such as muscle ripples,

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.950
skin wrinkles, and fur
interaction, are all added.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:18.040
All of these details are
too complex to hand-animate,

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.680
so they need simulations to generate them.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.190
The average first simulation,

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:25.090
for instance, took
eight hours to complete.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:26.474
One of the biggest improvements

00:06:26.474 --> 00:06:29.140
MPC Film wanted to make
since "The Jungle Book"

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.530
was how they portrayed
the layers of each animal,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.150
especially their muscles.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.223
Newman: Something that's
quite common in CG

00:06:36.223 --> 00:06:38.350
is that you kind of get
this water-balloon effect

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:39.900
when you simulate muscles.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:40.930
Narrator: This effect occurs

00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:43.150
when the muscles bounce around too much.

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.160
On the other hand,
animators also run the risk

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.390
of making the characters look too stiff.

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:49.344
Newman: The problem with that is

00:06:49.344 --> 00:06:50.793
because there's a lot of collisions

00:06:50.793 --> 00:06:51.710
happening under the skin,

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.340
that's quite difficult to simulate,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:56.480
but on "The Lion King" we
added hard surface joints,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.540
bones, basically, that
those muscles would collide

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.373
and slide against,

00:06:59.373 --> 00:07:02.830
and it reduced that sort
of water-balloon movement.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:04.700
Narrator: And even if we don't notice it,

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:06.280
there are several visual factors

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.310
to making the skin look just right,

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:11.970
like how light bounces off
of it and how it moves.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.970
Separate teams worked on each layer,

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:16.610
perhaps none being more
important than hair,

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:18.140
given that nearly every character

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:19.840
in the movie is covered in it.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:20.673
Part of the reason

00:07:20.673 --> 00:07:23.020
they really had to nail
things like muscles, bones,

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.720
and skin is because a
lion's hair is so short

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:26.730
that there's less to hide

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.770
how the skin and bones move beneath.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:31.130
Female lions have even less hair

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.670
than their male counterparts,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:34.510
and because they have no manes,

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:36.270
you can fully see their necks.

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:37.700
For characters like Nala,

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:41.130
they had to add certain movements
to the neck and esophagus

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.870
for moments when she's speaking.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:44.540
The male lions have manes,

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.780
which presented their
own set of challenges.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.410
Newman: Typically, the longer the hair is,

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:49.850
the harder it is to simulate.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.700
You get 1% of the whole
density of the groom,

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:53.670
you can then use that

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.360
to kind of approximate
the surrounding hairs

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.570
that move and collide with each other.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:07:59.510
Narrator: Adult Simba's
mane alone consisted

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:02.607
of about 700,000 strands of hair.

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:04.990
And the hairs can't just sit still.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:08.118
The animation team created new
systems for "The Lion King,"

00:08:08.118 --> 00:08:11.495
specifically for gusts of wind
passing through long hair.

00:08:11.495 --> 00:08:14.070
The hairs on a lion's
mane blowing in the wind

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.880
can be tricky to control,

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:18.170
so they made sure the strands
behind the leading hairs

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.240
in these shots wouldn't move as much,

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.430
helping to create a sense

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.000
that these animals were
covered in layers of hair.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.460
While extremely subtle,

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.040
Newman says it's the kind
of specific detail that,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.776
if not done correctly,
the viewer will notice.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:31.991
Newman: But it's that
kind of level of detail

00:08:31.991 --> 00:08:34.883
that we have to get to, to
kind of reach that kind of,

00:08:34.883 --> 00:08:37.290
the audience is believing
what they're looking at.

00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:40.360
Narrator: But sometimes they
actually needed less detail.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:42.101
Take, for example, the infamous

00:08:42.101 --> 00:08:44.334
wildebeest stampede sequence.

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:46.920
The animators started by
building up a big library

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.300
of animated clips of wildebeests
doing different actions,

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:54.140
whether that be walking,
jumping, or changing direction.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.123
This allowed them to more easily create

00:08:56.123 --> 00:08:58.170
a massive crowd in motion.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.790
The wildebeests in the distance were able

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.310
to have more simplified features

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.230
and could be done by simulation,

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:04.890
while they had to animate the wildebeests

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.690
that were closer to the camera,

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.860
and thus more visible to the eye.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.150
Of course, the world
surrounding the animals

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.630
was equally important.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:15.500
They built landscapes based
on many real-life locations

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:19.870
in places like Kenya,
Namibia, and California.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:21.280
Another piece of technology

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:22.756
they worked on since "The Jungle Book"

00:09:22.756 --> 00:09:25.550
was what Newman referred
to as a scatter tool.

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.780
It allowed animators to sprinkle elements

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.050
across the surface, like
twigs, leaves, and stones,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:34.000
instead of just placing
them there one by one.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.125
It's a big part of the reason

00:09:35.125 --> 00:09:37.300
"The Lion King" feels so realistic,

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.710
down to every tiny detail.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:42.630
One sequence really shows
off MPC's work creating

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.790
both the environments
and the animals in them:

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:47.362
"Circle of Life."

00:09:47.362 --> 00:09:49.640
Newman: I was on the movie for
about two and a half years,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:52.222
and we were working on that
sequence up until the end.

00:09:52.222 --> 00:09:53.904
It was really challenging.

00:09:53.904 --> 00:09:55.735
You know, just the scale of it

00:09:55.735 --> 00:09:57.430
and the complexity shot by shot.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.927
Every shot was slightly different

00:09:58.927 --> 00:10:00.457
in a slightly different part of the world.

00:10:00.457 --> 00:10:01.810
The light is different in every shot.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:03.610
Narrator: In an interview
with Vanity Fair,

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.210
Favreau cited an example

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:07.660
of a giraffe bowing
during "Circle of Life."

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:09.160
They would start with pencil drawings

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.050
of what they wanted the action to be

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.180
against reference footage

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.860
of an animal doing a similar action,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.580
and then hand-animate it.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:17.530
And there were a lot of bowing animals

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:18.960
in that sequence to coordinate,

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.830
and that's after getting
Rafiki to move correctly.

00:10:21.830 --> 00:10:23.930
The most surprising thing
about this sequence is that

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.895
it actually contains the
movie's only non-CGI shot.

00:10:26.895 --> 00:10:29.080
Favreau snuck in one real visual,

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.040
which was captured in Africa.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.290
Everything from the stones on the ground,

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:34.973
to the hairs on Simba,

00:10:34.973 --> 00:10:36.681
to the way Rafiki moved,

00:10:36.681 --> 00:10:39.020
to the way the cameras captured it all,

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:41.590
allowed for an experience in
which the audience felt like

00:10:41.590 --> 00:10:44.500
they were stepping into the
real-life African savanna,

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.310
while still spending time with
the same animated characters

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.893
we first fell in love with back in 1994.

